window.docContents[223].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Introduction--Release-history--April___-2023":{"contents":"This release adds an interlude on writing proofs with tactics as well as a final chapter that combines discussion of performance and cost models with proofs of termination and program equivalence.\nThis is the last release prior to the final release.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Introduction\u0009Release history","header":"April, 2023","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Introduction--Release-history--April___-2023"},"/#conditions-and-selection":{"contents":"Projection removes unwanted columns from a table, but queries must also be able to remove unwanted rows.\nThis operation is called selection.\nSelection relies on having a means of expressing which rows are desired.\n\nThe example query language contains expressions, which are analogous to what can be written in a WHERE clause in SQL.\nExpressions are represented by the indexed family .\nBecause expressions can refer to columns from the database, but different sub-expressions all have the same schema,  takes the database schema as a parameter.\nAdditionally, each expression has a type, and these vary, making it an index:\n\n\n\nThe  constructor represents a reference to a column in the database.\nThe  constructor compares two expressions for equality,  checks whether one is less than the other,  is Boolean conjunction, and  is a constant value of some type.\n\nFor example, an expression in  that checks whether the elevation column is greater than 1000 and the location is  can be written:\n\n\n\nThis is somewhat noisy.\nIn particular, references to columns contain boilerplate calls to .\nA Lean feature called macros can help make expressions easier to read by eliminating this boilerplate:\n\n\n\nThis declaration adds the c! keyword to Lean, and instructs Lean to replace any instance of c! followed by an expression with the corresponding  construction.\nHere,  stands for Lean expressions, rather than commands, tactics, or some other part of the language.\nLean macros are a bit like C preprocessor macros, except they are better integrated into the language and they automatically avoid some of the pitfalls of CPP.\nIn fact, they are very closely related to macros in Scheme and Racket.\n\nWith this macro, the expression can be much easier to read:\n\n\n\nFinding the value of an expression with respect to a given row uses  to extract column references, and it delegates to Lean's operations on values for every other expression:\n\n\n\nEvaluating the expression for Valby Bakke, the tallest hill in the Copenhagen area, yields  because Valby Bakke is much less than 1 km over sea level:\n\n\n\n\n\nEvaluating it for a fictional mountain of 1230m elevation yields :\n\n\n\n\n\nEvaluating it for the highest peak in the US state of Idaho yields , as Idaho is not part of Denmark:\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming with Dependent Types\u0009Worked Example: Typed Queries","header":"7.3.3. Conditions and Selection","id":"/#conditions-and-selection"},"/#conversion-to-strings":{"contents":"Another useful built-in class is called .\nInstances of  provide a standard way of converting values from a given type into strings.\nFor example, a  instance is used when a value occurs in an interpolated string, and it determines how the  function used at the beginning of the description of  will display a value.\n\nFor example, one way to convert a  into a  is to reveal its inner structure.\nThe function  takes a  that determines whether to parenthesize uses of , which should be  in the initial call to the function and  in all recursive calls.\n\n\n\nUsing this function for a  instance:\n\n\n\nresults in informative, yet overwhelming, output:\n\n\n\n\n\nOn the other hand, every positive number has a corresponding .\nConverting it to a  and then using the  instance (that is, the overloading of  for ) is a quick way to generate much shorter output:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWhen more than one instance is defined, the most recent takes precedence.\nAdditionally, if a type has a  instance, then it can be used to display the result of #eval so  outputs .\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Positive Numbers","header":"3.1.3. Conversion to Strings","id":"/#conversion-to-strings"}});